  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThis morning, the synoptic pattern across the CONUS was\ncharacterized by a stout upper high over the Four Corners\nregion, with the ridge axis extending over the Northern Rockies\nand into the southern Canadian Prairies. Surface high pressure\nwas centered in southwest Manitoba, with generally light and\nvariable winds that aid in the potential development of fog\nacross the forecast area this morning. A few automated sites in\nthe northwest are starting to report moderately reduced\nvisibility, but webcams look pretty clear, at least for now. \n\nUnderneath the ridge, a potent low-level thermal ridge extends \nacross the state, with 850mb temperatures as warm as 29 degrees \nC over southwest North Dakota. A Heat Advisory is in effect for \nmuch of western and central North Dakota this afternoon through \nSunday, with the exception of our northern two tiers of \ncounties. Ambient air temperatures will generally range from 95\nto 105 today, with heat indices at a similar level. \n\nHRRR smoke model output is similar to yesterday, although\nsky observations didn't seem to show quite as much smoke as we\nwould have expected based on levels the model was advertising.\nHowever, still carrying mention of patchy smoke, with some\npotential for minor surface impacts. Otherwise, mainly sunny\ntoday, just could be hazy sunshine depending on how thick the\nsmoke aloft ends up. \n\nA transient shortwave and weak surface low are progged to track\nthrough northern South Dakota, with low PoPs skirting our\nsouthern counties. SPC isolated thunderstorms area (level 1 out\nof 5) tags our extreme far southwest, but thinking any potential\nfor stronger to severe storms is pretty low, with deterministic\nguidance showing instability at 1000 J/kg or less. \n\nHot and dry weather continues on Sunday, with similar high\ntemperatures as today and the Heat Advisory continuing. Main\nforecast concern on Sunday is near critical fire weather\nconditions, generally for areas south and west of the Missouri\nRiver. Minimum relative humidity will be in the mid to upper\nteens for this area, but breezy northwest winds around 20 mph\nare enough to likely reach near critical conditions. \n\nNot many changes are expected looking ahead to the forecast for\nnext work week. NBM temperature percentile spreads are\nrelatively small, indicating high confidence in max temperatures\nstaying in the 90s, if not around 100 degrees F. There are on\nand off chances for showers and thunderstorms as embedded waves\nmove through and the ridge axis shifts around the region, but no\none day stands out in particular. There is potential for storms\nto be strong to severe during this period per NSSL and CSU\nmachine learning probabilities.",
